From 312bea9a0ab0a9eec2a863633afcb419e59f6dc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Siyuan Feng <sealseacat@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 23:34:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/12] feat(validation): PK-aligned completeness check; honest
 reporting; --strict gate
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The old masking-completeness check compared whole rows: a row counted as
unmasked only when EVERY column matched the source, so a row whose email
survived unmasked while the name changed was reported as fine. It also
answered 'PASSED' while quietly skipping things it could not verify.

- Tables with a primary key now get a direct check: rows aligned key-by-key,
  the sensitive column compared value-by-value. The case above is caught.
  Coverage is bounded by validation.pk_row_limit and the report states
  explicitly when it was partial.
- The old overlap heuristic remains only for keyless tables — and its clean
  result is now a WARNING ('cannot verify per-row without a primary key'),
  not a PASS. Identical whole rows still FAIL.
- Tables present on only one side are surfaced as warnings instead of being
  silently skipped.
- ValidationReport.passed_strict + 'dbmask validate --strict': warnings and
  skipped checks fail the gate. The non-strict summary line now names its
  caveats ('PASSED — with 2 warnings: not everything could be verified').
- FAIL details carry shape-redacted samples plus row keys — sensitive values
  no longer land in validation logs.

